/// A user's Google Calendar read for an assistant, as the native tools
/// `CalendarList`, `CalendarEvents`, `CalendarEvent` and `CalendarFreeBusy`,
/// each answered by `api-request` calls against the `oauth_provider` the
/// instance names, stamped with the calling conversation's owner and
/// compacted to what a model needs. The user's token never reaches this
/// component; `api-request` attaches it host-side. Every call is nested under
/// a request: an instance whose clauses say `user = "*"` cannot call on its
/// own account, so a caller with no user of its own is answered `denied`. Its
/// calls are Calendar's `calendarList.list`, `events.list`, `events.get` and
/// `freebusy.query`, all under the Calendar read scope set.
///
/// Every capability is load-bearing: `config` names the provider and its
/// host, without which no call can be addressed; `store` carries each
/// request's state between the activation that issues its nested call and the
/// one that receives the answer; `tools` is the authority to call
/// `api-request`; `provides` is what makes the tools exist; `log` records each
/// step's tool, status and size — never a body, a header or a query.
///
/// The instance's `config = { ... };` map is flat: `provider` is the
/// `oauth_provider` name; `calendar_host` is the `host[:port]` Calendar is
/// called on, which `api-request` matches against that provider's `api_hosts`
/// and the instance's clauses must admit. A missing or malformed key fails
/// every activation, naming the key.
///
/// Size the instance's result inbox with a `channel at
/// "brenn:tool-results/<slug>"` block: each live request has at most one
/// nested call outstanding, so the window must hold Σ max_concurrency results,
/// twice over for a re-delivered result — `retain_depth ≥ 2 × Σ`, rounded up
/// to a power of two. This class's Σ is 12, so 32 holds it; a tool joining
/// the class that takes Σ past 16 needs the block raised, and the family
/// default of 16 is too small for any class whose Σ is above 8.
///
/// Every nested answer crosses the bus as a tool result, so Google's body — a
/// JSON string inside `api-request`'s value, inside the result envelope — must
/// fit `[messaging] max_body_bytes` (64 KiB default). Either raise that cap to
/// carry the OAuth provider's `max_response_bytes` (256 KiB default) with a
/// margin for escaping, `messaging { max_body_bytes = 393216; }`, or lower
/// `max_response_bytes` to fit the default cap.
/// An answer over the bus cap never reaches this component as Google's answer;
/// the tools treat that refusal as they treat an answer cut at the read limit.
component GoogleCalendarRead {
  abi = processor;
  requires = [log, store, config, tools, provides];
  in tool-results;
  in tool-requests;
  provide calendar-list {
    mcp_name = "CalendarList";
    description = "The calendars in the user's Google Calendar list, in Google's order. Answers `calendars`, each with `id`, `summary` (the calendar's name; over 300 bytes (UTF-8) it is cut, ends in `…`, and the entry's `fields_truncated` lists it), `primary: true` on the user's own calendar, `access_role` (`owner`, `writer`, `reader` or `freeBusyReader`) and `time_zone`; and `next_page` when there are more than 100 — pass it back as `page`. `account` is needed only when the user has several Google accounts connected: the refusal lists them. An answer `{\"error\": \"credential_invalid\", …}` means this user has no usable Google credential: run `oauth-connect` for the provider with the Calendar read scope set the operator named and relay its steps, then `oauth-accounts` confirms. Gmail endpoints these tools do not cover (attachments, profile, history) and Calendar's colors and settings are listed with `api-request` examples in `docs/providers/google.md`.";
    input_schema = """{ "type": "object", "properties": { "page": { "type": "string", "minLength": 1, "maxLength": 4096, "pattern": "[^\\s\\u0085]" }, "account": { "type": "string", "minLength": 1, "maxLength": 320, "pattern": "[^\\s\\u0085]" } }, "additionalProperties": false }""";
    max_concurrency = 2;
  }
  provide calendar-events {
    mcp_name = "CalendarEvents";
    description = "The events on one of the user's Google calendars in the window `from` to `to` (each RFC 3339 with an offset, as `2026-11-02T09:00:00-05:00`; Google refuses a time without one, and a `to` not after `from`), recurring events expanded into their occurrences, in start order. `calendar` is a calendar `id` from `CalendarList`, default `primary` (the user's own calendar). Answers `events`, each with `id`, `summary`, `start` and `end` (`{date_time, time_zone?}` for a timed event, `{date}` for an all-day one, as Google gives them; nothing is converted), `location`, `status` (`confirmed`, `tentative` or `cancelled`), `organizer` (the organizer's email) and `self_response` (the answer to the invitation of the calendar read — `needsAction`, `declined`, `tentative` or `accepted` — when that calendar is an attendee: on `primary` the user's own, on another person's calendar that person's; a declined event is on that calendar but is not a commitment); `summary` and `location` over 300 bytes (UTF-8) and `organizer` over 320 are cut, end in `…`, and the event's `fields_truncated` lists them; `time_zone` (the calendar's); and `next_page` when there are more — pass it back as `page` with the same `calendar`, `from`, `to` and `q`. Other attendees are not listed: `CalendarEvent` reads one event with its attendees. `q` searches the events' text. `max` is how many events to return, 1 to 50, default 50. A window too large to read whole is refused as `internal`; the refusal says what to narrow. A refusal whose reason names neither `max_response_bytes` nor `max_body_bytes` is a transient fault, not a window too large: asking again with the same arguments may read the window whole. `account` is needed only when the user has several Google accounts connected: the refusal lists them. An answer `{\"error\": \"credential_invalid\", …}` means this user has no usable Google credential: run `oauth-connect` for the provider with the Calendar read scope set the operator named and relay its steps, then `oauth-accounts` confirms. Gmail endpoints these tools do not cover (attachments, profile, history) and Calendar's colors and settings are listed with `api-request` examples in `docs/providers/google.md`.";
    input_schema = """{ "type": "object", "properties": { "calendar": { "type": "string", "pattern": "^(?!\\.\\.?$)[^\\s/\\\\%\"\\x00-\\x1f\\x7f-\\x9f]{1,320}$" }, "from": { "type": "string", "minLength": 1, "maxLength": 64, "pattern": "[^\\s\\u0085]" }, "to": { "type": "string", "minLength": 1, "maxLength": 64, "pattern": "[^\\s\\u0085]" }, "q": { "type": "string", "minLength": 1, "maxLength": 1024, "pattern": "[^\\s\\u0085]" }, "max": { "type": "integer", "minimum": 1, "maximum": 50 }, "page": { "type": "string", "minLength": 1, "maxLength": 4096, "pattern": "[^\\s\\u0085]" }, "account": { "type": "string", "minLength": 1, "maxLength": 320, "pattern": "[^\\s\\u0085]" } }, "required": ["from", "to"], "additionalProperties": false }""";
    max_concurrency = 4;
  }
  provide calendar-event {
    mcp_name = "CalendarEvent";
    description = "One event of one of the user's Google calendars by `id` (an event `id` from `CalendarEvents`). `calendar` is the calendar `id` the event is on, from `CalendarList`, default `primary` (the user's own calendar); an event on another calendar is not found without it. Answers `id`, `summary`, `description` (as the organizer wrote it, which may contain HTML; it is not decoded), `start` and `end` (`{date_time, time_zone?}` for a timed event, `{date}` for an all-day one, as Google gives them; nothing is converted), `location`, `status` (`confirmed`, `tentative` or `cancelled`), `organizer` (`email`, `name`, and `self: true` when the organizer is the calendar read), `self_response` (the answer to the invitation of the calendar read — `needsAction`, `declined`, `tentative` or `accepted` — when that calendar is an attendee; a declined event is on that calendar but is not a commitment), `attendees` (each `email`, `name`, `response` and `optional: true` for an optional guest), `attendees_total` and `attendee_responses` (how many attendees `accepted`, `declined`, `tentative` and `needs_action`), `recurrence` (the recurring event's rules, RRULE lines, at most 20; `recurrence_truncated` when there are more), `recurring_event_id` (the recurring event an occurrence belongs to) and `conference` (the video meeting link), each when present. The calendar read is the user on `primary` and another person on that person's calendar: there `organizer.self` and `self_response` are that person's, and the user's own response is the user's entry in `attendees`. An event with more than 50 attendees is answered with the calendar read's own entry alone and `attendees_omitted: true`; `attendees_total` and `attendee_responses` are given only when every attendee is listed. `summary`, `location`, the organizer's `name`, each `recurrence` line and `conference` over 1000 bytes (UTF-8), an attendee's `name` over 300, and an `email` over 320 are cut, end in `…`, and the object's `fields_truncated` lists them. `max_chars` (200 to 60000, default 12000) bounds `description`: it is cut at `max_chars` characters, and shorter when the whole answer would otherwise exceed the wrapper's result ceiling; `description_truncated` and `description_chars` (the whole description's length in characters) say so either way. An event too large to read whole is refused as `internal`, naming the provider's `max_response_bytes` or the bus's `max_body_bytes`; a refusal naming neither is a transient fault, and reading the event again may answer it. `account` is needed only when the user has several Google accounts connected: the refusal lists them. An answer `{\"error\": \"credential_invalid\", …}` means this user has no usable Google credential: run `oauth-connect` for the provider with the Calendar read scope set the operator named and relay its steps, then `oauth-accounts` confirms. Gmail endpoints these tools do not cover (attachments, profile, history) and Calendar's colors and settings are listed with `api-request` examples in `docs/providers/google.md`.";
    input_schema = """{ "type": "object", "properties": { "id": { "type": "string", "pattern": "^[A-Za-z0-9_-]{1,1024}$" }, "calendar": { "type": "string", "pattern": "^(?!\\.\\.?$)[^\\s/\\\\%\"\\x00-\\x1f\\x7f-\\x9f]{1,320}$" }, "max_chars": { "type": "integer", "minimum": 200, "maximum": 60000 }, "account": { "type": "string", "minLength": 1, "maxLength": 320, "pattern": "[^\\s\\u0085]" } }, "required": ["id"], "additionalProperties": false }""";
    max_concurrency = 4;
  }
  provide calendar-free-busy {
    mcp_name = "CalendarFreeBusy";
    description = "When the user's Google calendars are busy in the window `from` to `to` (each RFC 3339 with an offset, as `2026-11-02T09:00:00-05:00`; Google refuses a time without one, and a `to` not after `from`). `calendars` is a list of 1 to 50 distinct calendar `id`s from `CalendarList`, default `[\"primary\"]` (the user's own calendar); the ids together may hold at most 48000 bytes of UTF-8, and more is refused: ask for fewer. Answers `from` and `to` (the window as Google read it) and `calendars`, keyed by each calendar `id` asked, each with `busy` (the busy intervals, each `start` and `end` as Google gives them; a time not in one is free) or `errors` (Google's reasons it could not answer for that calendar, such as `notFound`; the calendar's busy times are then unknown, not free). Event details are not included: read them with `CalendarEvents`. A window too large to read or answer whole is refused as `internal`; the refusal says what to narrow. A refusal that says the host did not deliver api-request's answer and names neither `max_response_bytes` nor `max_body_bytes` is a transient fault, not a window too large: asking again with the same arguments may read the window whole. `account` is needed only when the user has several Google accounts connected: the refusal lists them. An answer `{\"error\": \"credential_invalid\", …}` means this user has no usable Google credential: run `oauth-connect` for the provider with the Calendar read scope set the operator named and relay its steps, then `oauth-accounts` confirms. Gmail endpoints these tools do not cover (attachments, profile, history) and Calendar's colors and settings are listed with `api-request` examples in `docs/providers/google.md`.";
    input_schema = """{ "type": "object", "properties": { "from": { "type": "string", "minLength": 1, "maxLength": 64, "pattern": "[^\\s\\u0085]" }, "to": { "type": "string", "minLength": 1, "maxLength": 64, "pattern": "[^\\s\\u0085]" }, "calendars": { "type": "array", "minItems": 1, "maxItems": 50, "uniqueItems": true, "items": { "type": "string", "pattern": "^(?!\\.\\.?$)[^\\s/\\\\%\"\\x00-\\x1f\\x7f-\\x9f]{1,320}$" } }, "account": { "type": "string", "minLength": 1, "maxLength": 320, "pattern": "[^\\s\\u0085]" } }, "required": ["from", "to"], "additionalProperties": false }""";
    max_concurrency = 2;
  }
}
